.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt SSL_SESSION_GET_TIME 3ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm SSL_SESSION_get_time ,
.Nm SSL_SESSION_set_time ,
.Nm SSL_SESSION_get_timeout ,
.Nm SSL_SESSION_set_timeout ,
.Nm SSL_SESSION_get_time_ex ,
.Nm SSL_SESSION_set_time_ex ,
.Nm SSL_get_time ,
.Nm SSL_set_time ,
.Nm SSL_get_timeout ,
.Nm SSL_set_timeout
.Nd retrieve and manipulate session time and timeout settings
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/ssl.h
.Ft long
.Fo SSL_SESSION_get_timeout
.Fa "const SSL_SESSION *s"
.Fc
.Ft long
.Fo SSL_SESSION_set_timeout
.Fa "SSL_SESSION *s"
.Fa "long tm"
.Fc
.Ft long
.Fo SSL_get_timeout
.Fa "const SSL_SESSION *s"
.Fc
.Ft long
.Fo SSL_set_timeout
.Fa "SSL_SESSION *s"
.Fa "long tm"
.Fc
.Ft time_t
.Fo SSL_SESSION_get_time_ex
.Fa "const SSL_SESSION *s"
.Fc
.Ft time_t
.Fo SSL_SESSION_set_time_ex
.Fa "SSL_SESSION *s"
.Fa "time_t tm"
.Fc
The following functions have been deprecated since OpenSSL 3.4, and can
be hidden entirely by defining
.Ar OPENSSL_API_COMPAT
with a suitable version value, see
.Xr openssl_user_macros 7 :
.Ft long
.Fo SSL_SESSION_get_time
.Fa "const SSL_SESSION *s"
.Fc
.Ft long
.Fo SSL_SESSION_set_time
.Fa "SSL_SESSION *s"
.Fa "long tm"
.Fc
.Ft long
.Fo SSL_get_time
.Fa "const SSL_SESSION *s"
.Fc
.Ft long
.Fo SSL_set_time
.Fa "SSL_SESSION *s"
.Fa "long tm"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn SSL_SESSION_get_time_ex
returns the time at which the session
.Fa s
was established.
The time is given in seconds since the Epoch and therefore compatible to
the time delivered by the
.Xr time 3
call.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_SESSION_set_time_ex
replaces the creation time of the session
.Fa s
with the chosen value
.Fa tm .
.Pp
.Fn SSL_SESSION_get_timeout
returns the timeout value set for session
.Fa s
in seconds.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_SESSION_set_timeout
sets the timeout value for session
.Fa s
in seconds to
.Fa tm .
.Pp
.Fn SSL_SESSION_get_time
and
.Fn SSL_SESSION_set_time
functions use the long datatype instead of time_t and are therefore
deprecated due to not being Y2038-safe on 32 bit systems.
Note that such systems still need to be configured to use 64 bit time_t
to be able to avoid overflow in system time.
.Pp
The
.Fn SSL_get_time ,
.Fn SSL_set_time ,
.Fn SSL_get_timeout ,
and
.Fn SSL_set_timeout
functions are synonyms for the
.Xr SSL_SESSION_* 3
counterparts.
.Sh NOTES
Sessions are expired by examining the creation time and the timeout
value.
Both are set at creation time of the session to the actual time and the
default timeout value at creation, respectively, as set by
.Xr SSL_CTX_set_timeout 3 .
Using these functions it is possible to extend or shorten the lifetime
of the session.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn SSL_SESSION_get_time_ex
and
.Fn SSL_SESSION_get_timeout
return the currently valid values.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_SESSION_set_time_ex
returns time on success.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_SESSION_set_timeout
returns 1 on success.
.Pp
If any of the function is passed the NULL pointer for the session
.Fa s ,
0 is returned.
.Sh BUGS
The data type long is typically 32 bits on many systems, hence the old
functions
.Fn SSL_SESSION_get_time
and
.Fn SSL_SESSION_set_time
are not always Y2038 safe.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ssl 7 ,
.Xr SSL_CTX_set_timeout 3 ,
.Xr SSL_get_default_timeout 3
.Sh HISTORY
The functions
.Fn SSL_SESSION_get_time_ex
and
.Fn SSL_SESSION_set_time_ex
were added in OpenSSL 3.3.
